<section
  id='case-portfolio'
  class='fullscreen-section case-study-section'
  data-title='Case Study: Personal Portfolio — Web Development | Katja Turnsek'
  data-description='Creative portfolio case study focused on modular JavaScript, Handlebars, client-side routing, responsive images, theme switching, and accessible GSAP motion.'
  data-case='portfolio'
  aria-labelledby='case-portfolio-title'
>
  <div class='rows'>
    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-header'>
        <p class='case-eyebrow'>Web Development</p>
        <h1 id='case-portfolio-title'>Katja Dev Portfolio</h1>
        <p class='case-subtitle'>A custom portfolio where visual identity, interaction, and
          front-end architecture work as one system.</p>

        <dl class='case-summary' aria-label='Project summary'>
          <div>
            <dt>Role</dt>
            <dd>UX/UI design and front-end development</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Scope</dt>
            <dd>Independent portfolio project</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Year</dt>
            <dd>2025–2026</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Tools</dt>
            <dd>HTML, CSS, JavaScript, Handlebars, GSAP, Vite</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Outcome</dt>
            <dd>Live evolving portfolio</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>

        <div class='tech-demo-wrapper case-demo-wrapper'>
          <div class='demo-links' data-auto='demo' data-case='portfolio'></div>
        </div>

        <nav
          class='project-switcher'
          aria-label='Katja Dev Portfolio sections'
          data-auto='switcher'
          data-case='portfolio'
        ></nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>The brief</h2>
      <p>
        I wanted the portfolio itself to demonstrate how I work between design and code. It needed
        to support a growing collection of development, interaction-design, and graphic-design cases
        while retaining a distinct visual identity, smooth navigation, responsive behavior, and
        sensible performance across devices.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-grid'>
        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--challenge'>
          <h2>Challenge</h2>
          <p>
            The expressive motion and fullscreen navigation had to feel playful without obscuring
            the work or making the site fragile. At the same time, adding new project variants
            needed to remain manageable rather than requiring duplicated navigation and link markup.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Balance animated visual character with readability and control</li>
            <li>Keep routes, project switchers, and demo links consistent as the portfolio grows</li>
            <li>Serve appropriate imagery and theme assets without slowing the first view</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--desktop'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/site-portfolio-600.webp'
            data-src='site-portfolio-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='400'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Katja Dev Portfolio desktop homepage with typography, work navigation, and animated visual elements'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>Desktop homepage and visual identity</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--decisions'>
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Split the app into Handlebars partials and focused JavaScript modules for routing,
              navigation, work data, responsive images, themes, and animation behavior
            </li>
            <li>
              Made project data the shared source for work cards, case switchers, and external links
              to reduce repeated content and mismatched states
            </li>
            <li>
              Used GSAP for the signature waves, transitions, and liquid-inspired blobs while
              respecting reduced-motion preferences
            </li>
            <li>
              Combined responsive image loading, deferred heavy animation, and a Vite build to keep
              the visual experience practical on smaller devices
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--mobile'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/site-portfoliomobile-600.webp'
            data-src='site-portfoliomobile-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='490'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Katja Dev Portfolio mobile homepage and fullscreen navigation menu'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>Responsive homepage and fullscreen menu</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row case-outcome'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>Outcome and reflection</h2>
      <p>
        The portfolio has become both a presentation layer and an ongoing front-end project. It
        reflects my visual background while demonstrating modular JavaScript, routing, reusable
        data-driven components, responsive assets, theme logic, and interaction design. Continuing
        to refine it has also taught me when motion adds personality—and when simplifying an effect
        makes the work itself stronger.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='back-to-work-container'>
      <a
        href='{{base}}#work'
        class='label-link back-to-work-label'
        data-back
        data-back-fallback='{{base}}#work'
        aria-label='Back to Work'
      >
        <svg class='icon icon-18 arrow-icon' aria-hidden='true'><use href='#i-arrow-left' /></svg>
        <span class='label-text'>Back to Work</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>